m the likes of Sony  Microsoft and Boeing but remained  fiercely loyal to her vision as she  shared here on 60 minutes if people  could see the kind of office I've had to  use Harry in advertising and publicity  and all sorts of ridiculous frankly  things and I've said no to absolutely  all of them but there was one offer she  couldn't refuse from Hollywood and one  studio was about to discover that Harry  Potter might just work miracles I was  working for a new production company for  David Heyman who'd said to me go and  find me something for the studio well I  read an article in a newspaper that said  that someone had written the first in a  seven series book about a little boy who  goes to boarding school and discovers  he's a wizard so when I went and met her  agent and tried to convince him to let  us buy it we were coming as Warner  Brothers  halda Garabedian is president of the box  office division of hollywood.com  i paid for warner brothers to get the  Harry Potter franchise was a mission  I mean Warner Brothers had had great  success with the Batman franchise with  the Matrix franchise and he they were  determined to do it and I think that's  why they gave JK Rowling such latitude  in terms of her control over this  process and over the series of films our  first meeting with JK Rowling was over  lunch actually David Heyman and I had  told her a little bit about what we  wanted to do with the film and she sat  down with us and we tried desperately  hard to impress her and she of course  didn't need to impress us cuz she would  he have done  JK Rowling was paid reportedly two  million dollars by Warner Brothers for  the rights to those first four films  there are not many authors who keep  putting out books in a series that can  sell 10 12 15 million copies on the  first day of publication that gave her a  lot of leverage that leverage allowed  her to demand that the film be shot in  Britain with an all British cast and  that she would have a role in choosing  both the actors and directors top  directors clamored for admission to  Rowland's imaginary world well I know  that Steven Spielberg was approached to  direct the first Harry Potter film and  he actually wanted to do it as an  animated film but that didn't come to  pass and so Chris Columbus stepped in  producer David Heyman introduced the  hand-picked cast in a Warner Brothers  video versus choice of three wonderful  British actors for the leading roles  shows how well he understands the spirit  of these books I think her primary  concern about a film being made of her  vision was that we were true and  faithful to the books I think she had  really worked incredibly hard and  constructing characters in a story that  she believed in and what she didn't want  to see was that being chopped up and  misinterpreted what  right absent the biggest challenge of  bringing JK Rowling's vision to the  screen was that by the time we were  filming it everybody had read it  could you ever imagine that would be  like this  oh never in a million years I mean this  was the last thing I expected the first  Harry Potter film hit movie screens in  November 2001 once again the boy wizard  worked wonders the film was a  record-breaking hit it opened ninety  point three million dollars which turned  out to be the biggest opening weekend of  that year the film went on to earn three  hundred and seventeen million dollars  which was the biggest earner  domestically at the box office in 2001  Rowling not only retained creative  control she negotiated an undisclosed  percentage of the movie and licensing  profits more important she was happy  with the final product  I guess my abiding memory of her  reaction to the completed films was we  did it it's all right we didn't let her  down  2001 was also the year Joanne Rowling  happily married her second husband dr.  Neil Michael Murray and the Harry Potter  movies continued to be blockbusters  every Harry Potter movie has opened at  number one it has a perfect opening  weekend track record we're talking about  a decade we're talking about seven films  at the at Racal box office for the seven  Harry Potter films and this doesn't even  include part two of Deathly Hallows is  six point four billion dollars that  doesn't even include home video product  tie-ins any kind of soundtrack sales  anything else it's the biggest movie  franchise of all time  Roli also receives a cut of Mattel's 200  million dollar-plus revenues in Harry  Potter toys and her merchandising Empire  continues to expand it's hard to think  of something that didn't at one point or  another have a Harry Potter image  attached to it whether it's in the games  business the toys even clothing business  its humongously successful in 2010 Harry  Potter took claim of its own theme park  at Universal Studios in Orlando named  the Wizarding World of Harry Potter and  once again Rowling's personal stamp was  on everything from the snow on the roof  to the taste of the butterbeer they  actually pack up the ingredients went up  to Edinburgh made it and presented it to  her and she tasted them all and said  that one that one is exactly butterbeer  she's had two more children and now  makes her home on a pastoral estate in  northern Scotland and five years after  the last Potter book Rowling published a  new novel for adults I have gone from  dragons and unicorns and all the fun  that's involved in writing that to a  book that's intensely personal that  expresses a lot of my reality the Casual  Vacancy didn't have the same magic with  critics but it still hit number one on  the bestsellers lists Rowling has  redefined success in the literary world  her net worth is estimated to be near 1  billion dollars making her among the  richest women in the world  she has become a wonderfully elevated  brand in terms of stature and ethics and  the way that she conducts herself her  generosity extends to charities that  include aid to single-parent children  the poor and to multiple sclerosis  research for 17 years  Rowling lived and breathed Harry Potter  her story much like his was marked by  overcoming dreadful challenges on the  way to extraordinary success as she told  the Harvard graduating class in 2008 the  knowledge that you have emerged wiser  and stronger from setbacks means that  you are ever after secure in your  ability to survive you will never truly  know yourself or the strength of your  relationships until both have been  tested by adversity such knowledge is a  true gift for all that it is painfully  won and it has been worth more than any  qualification I ever earned
 

